  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in the District of Columbia is concentrated and sizable in FY2026 to date. Total obligations are $20.5B across 31,931 awards, with an average award size of $643K. Obligations fell 34.7% year over year. Annual obligations were higher in prior years, with FY24 at $36B and FY23 at $33.9B, and they have trended lower into FY26. The Department of Defense is the single largest buyer at $4B, followed by the Department of Transportation at $2.8B and the General Services Administration at $2.7B. Technology and professional services dominate the market. Computer Systems Design Services leads industries with $3B, Engineering Services follows with $2.5B, and Other Computer Related Services totals $2.3B. Large prime contractors include BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $800M and HARRIS CORPORATION at $711M, with several consulting and technology firms in the top ten. This mix matters for contractors deciding where to pursue opportunities and which agencies and NAICS to prioritize during the remaining FY2026 period.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal contracting in the District of Columbia?",
        "body": "Spending is concentrated: total obligations are $20.5B across 31,931 awards, with an average award size of $643K. A handful of agencies account for a large share of that total, and the top industries are technology and consulting services. Obligations fell 34.7% year over year, reflecting a smaller FY2026 to date total compared with recent fiscal years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries should contractors watch?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer at $4B, then the Department of Transportation at $2.8B and the General Services Administration at $2.7B. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry at $3B, with Engineering Services at $2.5B and Other Computer Related Services at $2.3B. Prime opportunities are clustered among major vendors such as BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC and HARRIS CORPORATION."
      }
    ]
